---
/*
 * Het privacybeleid, op /privacy/.
 *
 * Deze pagina bestaat omdat App Store Connect een Privacy Policy URL eist voor
 * elke app in de store. Ze is met opzet kaal: geen BaseLayout, geen huisstijl,
 * geen CSS, geen scripts en geen GA4-tag. Platte tekst, want dat is wat een
 * beleid moet zijn en er valt niets aan te ontwerpen.
 *
 * Noindex, want ze hoort niet in de zoekresultaten van een fansite (Nutri,
 * 17 september 2026). Ze staat daarom ook niet in de sitemap; de uitzondering
 * staat bij de filter in `astro.config.mjs`, naast die van de inzendpagina.
 *
 * Eén taal. De site doet er zes, maar App Store Connect wil één adres en een
 * beleid vertalen betekent zes teksten die uit elkaar kunnen lopen; dan is één
 * juiste versie beter dan zes die niet meer gelijk zijn. Engels, de
 * standaardtaal van de site.
 *
 * Geen bedrijfsgegevens, geen naam en geen adres in de tekst (§2). Contact
 * loopt via de inzendpagina, die er al is.
 *
 * Wat hier staat hoort overeen te komen met `PrivacyInfo.xcprivacy` in de app
 * en met het privacyformulier in App Store Connect. Verandert er iets aan wat
 * de app of de site bewaart, dan verandert het op die drie plekken samen.
 * De nieuwsbrief (6 oktober 2026) is alleen de site, niet de app: die raakt
 * het privacyformulier van App Store Connect en de app dus niet.
 */
const updated = '6 October 2026';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <meta name="robots" content="noindex, nofollow" />
    <title>Privacy Policy - Megamundo</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <h1>Megamundo Privacy Policy</h1>

    <p>Last updated: {updated}</p>

    <p>
      Megamundo is an independent fan site about Grand Theft Auto. It is not
      affiliated with, endorsed by, or connected to Rockstar Games or Take-Two
      Interactive. This policy covers the website at megamundo.be and the
      Megamundo app for iPhone and iPad.
    </p>

    <p>
      The short version: there are no accounts, you are never asked for your
      name or any other personal detail, your email address is only asked for
      if you choose to subscribe to the newsletter, and nothing you do here is
      sold or handed to advertisers.
    </p>

    <h2>1. No account, ever</h2>
    <p>
      You cannot sign up and you cannot log in. Reading the news, liking an
      article, watching a clip and receiving a notification all work without an
      identity. Nothing on this site or in the app asks who you are. The one
      thing that needs an email address is the newsletter, and only because a
      newsletter has to be sent somewhere.
    </p>

    <h2>2. The app</h2>

    <h3>Notifications</h3>
    <p>
      If you allow notifications, Apple gives the app a push token for your
      device. That token is stored together with the language you read in and
      whether the app is a test build or a store build, and it is used for one
      thing only: sending you a short message when a new article is published.
      The token points to a device, not to a person, and it is never used for
      anything else. Turning notifications off in iOS Settings, or deleting the
      app, stops it from working; the token then expires on Apple's side and
      is removed.
    </p>

    <h3>Likes</h3>
    <p>
      The first time you like an article, the app generates a random identifier
      and keeps it on your device. That identifier is sent along with each like
      so that the same device cannot like the same article twice. It is not
      derived from your device, your Apple Account or anything about you, and
      it is not connected to the push token or to any other data. Deleting the
      app deletes it.
    </p>

    <h3>Settings</h3>
    <p>
      Your language, your choice between day and night mode, and the list of
      articles you liked stay on your device and are not sent anywhere.
    </p>

    <h3>No analytics</h3>
    <p>
      The app contains no analytics, no advertising identifier and no tracking
      of any kind, and it shares nothing with third parties beyond what is
      described above.
    </p>

    <h2>3. The website</h2>

    <h3>Visitor statistics</h3>
    <p>
      The website uses Google Analytics to count visits and see which articles
      are read. Google sets its own cookies for this and receives your IP
      address. The reports we see are totals, not individuals. The app does not
      use Google Analytics.
    </p>

    <h3>Likes</h3>
    <p>
      Liking an article in a browser works the same way as in the app: a random
      identifier is stored in your browser and sent with the like. Clearing
      your browser data removes it.
    </p>

    <h3>Embedded clips and streams</h3>
    <p>
      Clips and streams are played by YouTube and Twitch inside their own
      players. Those players set their own cookies and follow the privacy
      policies of Google and Amazon respectively, over which we have no
      control.
    </p>

    <h3>Posts from X in articles</h3>
    <p>
      An article that reports on a post from X shows that post as X publishes
      it. Only those articles load a script from X; every other page on this
      site does not. The embed sets its own cookies, sees your IP address and
      follows the privacy policy of X, over which we have no control. We ask X
      not to personalise the embed, but we cannot enforce that. Blocking
      scripts from x.com leaves the text of the post and a link to the
      original in place, so nothing is lost.
    </p>

    <h3>Posts from Reddit in articles</h3>
    <p>
      The same goes for a post from Reddit. Only an article that shows one
      loads a script from Reddit, which sets its own cookies, sees your IP
      address and follows the privacy policy of Reddit, over which we have no
      control. Blocking scripts from reddit.com leaves the title of the post,
      who posted it and a link to the original in place.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Sending in news</h2>
    <p>
      If you use the form to send in a tip, what you type in it is stored: the
      headline, the optional link to the source, an optional note, and the name
      or nickname you choose to add. The name field is optional and exists only
      so you can be credited. Tips are read by a person and are not published
      automatically.
    </p>

    <h2>5. The newsletter</h2>
    <p>
      If you subscribe to the newsletter on the website, we store your email
      address, the language of the page you subscribed on, the time you
      subscribed, and the random browser identifier described above, which is
      used only to limit how often one browser can sign up. The address is used
      for one thing: sending you the newsletter. It is never shared, sold or
      used for advertising. The app does not ask for an email address.
    </p>
    <p>
      Sending has not started yet. When it does, the list will be handled by an
      email service acting on our behalf, and every newsletter will contain a
      link to unsubscribe. Until then, you can unsubscribe or have your address
      removed through the contact form below.
    </p>
    <h2>6. Where the data is kept</h2>
    <p>
      The pages, the articles and the images are static files served by GitHub
      Pages. Likes, push tokens, tips and newsletter addresses are stored in a
      hosted PostgreSQL database provided by Supabase. Notifications are
      delivered by Apple. These providers process the data on our behalf;
      none of them receive it for their own purposes.
    </p>

    <h2>7. How long it is kept</h2>
    <p>
      A like is a number in a counter and stays as long as the article exists.
      A push token is kept until it stops working or you turn notifications
      off. A tip is kept until it has been dealt with. A newsletter address is
      kept until you unsubscribe or ask for it to be removed.
    </p>

    <h2>8. Children</h2>
    <p>
      Megamundo is not aimed at children under 13 and does not knowingly
      collect anything from them. Apart from a newsletter address someone
      chooses to give, nothing here identifies anyone.
    </p>

    <h2>9. Your rights</h2>
    <p>
      Under the GDPR you can ask what is held about you, and ask for it to be
      corrected or erased. Because there are no accounts and no identifiers
      that point back to a person, there is in practice nothing to look up: we
      cannot tell which likes came from your device, and we would not be able
      to find them even if you asked. You can remove everything from your side
      yourself, by turning notifications off, clearing your browser data, or
      deleting the app.
    </p>
    <p>
      The newsletter is the exception: if you subscribed, your address can be
      found, and you can ask through the contact form to see it, correct it or
      have it erased.
    </p>

    <h2>10. Changes</h2>
    <p>
      If this policy changes, the date at the top changes with it.
    </p>

    <h2>11. Contact</h2>
    <p>
      Questions about this policy, and requests about your newsletter address,
      can be sent through the form at
      <a href="https://megamundo.be/submit/">megamundo.be/submit</a>.
    </p>
  </body>
</html>
